<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hellenica Oxyrhynchia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hellenica Oxyrhynchia is a Greek historical work known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> fragments found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and first published in 1908. Its unknown author continued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>, and the surviving parts cover events of 409 and 396–395 BCE in detail. It is valued as a careful, independent source alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> and is the main evidence for the federal constitution of the Boeotian League.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hellenica-oxyrhynchia.jpg" alt="Hellenica Oxyrhynchia" width="320" height="546" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Издательство Кларендон, Оксфорд, 1913 г. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HellenicaOxyrhynchia_Walker.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
